1
00:00:25,959 --> 00:00:28,193
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,336
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,483
♪ ♪

4
00:01:05,032 --> 00:01:07,966
♪ ♪

5
00:01:36,063 --> 00:01:38,197
♪ ♪

6
00:01:58,085 --> 00:02:00,552
♪ ♪

7
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:32,384
♪ ♪

8
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:42,828
Somebody tried to run you over?

9
00:02:42,863 --> 00:02:44,162
Yeah.

10
00:02:44,197 --> 00:02:47,099
I have no idea who.

11
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:48,633
How is he?

12
00:02:48,668 --> 00:02:49,812
Fine.

13
00:02:49,836 --> 00:02:51,748
Just a little doped
up, that's all.

14
00:02:51,772 --> 00:02:53,071
They just want

15
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:55,307
to keep him under
observation for a while.

16
00:02:55,342 --> 00:02:57,276
Any leads on Shelby?

17
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:01,547
I lost her.

18
00:03:01,582 --> 00:03:04,183
Oh.

19
00:03:04,218 --> 00:03:08,053
She was so close,
and I lost her.

20
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:10,088
Hmm.

21
00:03:11,625 --> 00:03:14,192
She's not gonna show
up at the trial, is she?

22
00:03:14,228 --> 00:03:17,261
Not looking like it.

23
00:03:17,297 --> 00:03:18,596
It's all my fault.

24
00:03:18,632 --> 00:03:20,732
We could have
had a case, but I...

25
00:03:20,767 --> 00:03:23,602
I let her disappear.

26
00:03:25,171 --> 00:03:26,972
If she just hadn't
been a witness

27
00:03:27,007 --> 00:03:29,741
at the preliminary, but she was.

28
00:03:29,776 --> 00:03:32,077
So her testimony can, must

29
00:03:32,112 --> 00:03:34,012
and will be
introduced at the trial,

30
00:03:34,047 --> 00:03:35,580
and we can't even challenge it.

31
00:03:35,616 --> 00:03:38,182
If I could have just
asked her a few questions

32
00:03:38,218 --> 00:03:41,586
at the preliminary...
We can object

33
00:03:41,622 --> 00:03:44,455
that the district attorney
hasn't really tried to find her,

34
00:03:44,491 --> 00:03:46,557
but he has to be
looking for her.

35
00:03:46,593 --> 00:03:48,526
He'd be a fool not to be.

36
00:03:50,163 --> 00:03:52,263
Diplomatic immunity
may be the only way

37
00:03:52,298 --> 00:03:53,632
out of this for him.

38
00:03:53,667 --> 00:03:57,502
I'd sooner that
car had killed me.

39
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:03,474
Say No To Sweets.

40
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:06,511
<i>Sugar-Free Cooking.</i>

41
00:04:06,546 --> 00:04:07,979
Hmm.

42
00:04:08,014 --> 00:04:10,682
Looks like Shelby Russell
was concerned about her figure.

43
00:04:10,717 --> 00:04:13,018
Autopsy said her
husband was a diabetic.

44
00:04:13,053 --> 00:04:16,893
There's lots of books
on antiques, too.

45
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:19,491
Oh, wait a minute.
Hold the phone.

46
00:04:19,526 --> 00:04:22,827
What is it?

47
00:04:22,863 --> 00:04:24,929
A monthly statement.

48
00:04:24,964 --> 00:04:26,931
Bills for somebody

49
00:04:26,966 --> 00:04:29,526
staying at Miles-Carson
Convalescent Home.

50
00:04:30,804 --> 00:04:32,738
Elizabeth Farrell.
Now, who's this?

51
00:04:34,942 --> 00:04:36,875
Shelby's mother?

52
00:04:37,945 --> 00:04:39,122
I'm going over there.

53
00:04:39,146 --> 00:04:40,578
What should I do?

54
00:04:40,614 --> 00:04:41,780
Find Tyler.

55
00:04:41,815 --> 00:04:43,514
Right.

56
00:04:54,361 --> 00:04:56,805
Tyler?

57
00:04:56,829 --> 00:04:58,074
Find her?

58
00:04:58,098 --> 00:04:59,464
Nada.

59
00:04:59,499 --> 00:05:01,433
Nothing. No lead, no trail.

60
00:05:01,468 --> 00:05:03,702
The police have nothing.

61
00:05:03,737 --> 00:05:05,003
Where's Ben?

62
00:05:05,038 --> 00:05:07,016
He went to visit her mother.

63
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:08,606
Her mother, huh?

64
00:05:08,642 --> 00:05:10,374
Can I ask you a question?

65
00:05:10,410 --> 00:05:12,722
Why do you think Mike
Russell was murdered?

66
00:05:12,746 --> 00:05:15,513
That I don't know,
but those three guys...

67
00:05:15,548 --> 00:05:17,249
Baldwin, Kramer, Clark.

68
00:05:17,284 --> 00:05:20,485
Those three did it, or
at least one of them did.

69
00:05:20,521 --> 00:05:22,065
A business deal
gone sour, right?

70
00:05:22,089 --> 00:05:25,056
See, now, that's
what I think, too.

71
00:05:25,091 --> 00:05:27,726
You know, we got to get inside
the pharmaceutical company.

72
00:05:27,761 --> 00:05:29,060
I can't get in there.

73
00:05:29,095 --> 00:05:31,196
All three of the
partners have seen me.

74
00:05:31,231 --> 00:05:33,276
They haven't seen me.

75
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,400
You mean, you want to go in?

76
00:05:35,436 --> 00:05:38,102
Yeah. What do you think?

77
00:05:38,138 --> 00:05:40,938
Nah.

78
00:05:46,847 --> 00:05:49,114
Yo, taxi!

79
00:05:50,483 --> 00:05:53,043
Two blocks over!

80
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:03,954
Where you think you're going?

81
00:06:05,165 --> 00:06:07,099
I'm expected.

82
00:06:09,269 --> 00:06:10,446
Wait here.

83
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:12,403
Right.

84
00:06:25,452 --> 00:06:26,896
Sit down. What'll you have?

85
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:30,555
I thought this was going to be
a meeting with all three of you.

86
00:06:30,591 --> 00:06:32,557
Please, sit.

87
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:33,869
Pasquale, bring
him the manicotti.

88
00:06:33,893 --> 00:06:36,861
Is that okay with you,
Mister, uh... David Long?

89
00:06:36,896 --> 00:06:39,363
Yeah, that'd-that'd be fine,
Pasquale. That... Great.

90
00:06:39,399 --> 00:06:40,632
Thank you.

91
00:06:42,235 --> 00:06:44,002
Shouldn't we wait
for your partners?

92
00:06:44,037 --> 00:06:45,848
I speak for all of us.

93
00:06:45,872 --> 00:06:48,940
Now, what is this, uh,
proposition of yours?

94
00:06:48,975 --> 00:06:51,842
Well, as-as...

95
00:06:51,878 --> 00:06:53,945
As I said to you over the phone,

96
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:56,481
I used to be Michael
Russell's accountant.

97
00:06:56,516 --> 00:06:58,416
I know all about the
pharmaceutical company,

98
00:06:58,452 --> 00:06:59,750
the silent partnership,

99
00:06:59,786 --> 00:07:02,687
and I-I know just how
profitable your partnership is.

100
00:07:02,722 --> 00:07:04,000
What's your point?

101
00:07:04,024 --> 00:07:05,189
Well...

102
00:07:07,427 --> 00:07:08,626
Michael's dead.

103
00:07:08,662 --> 00:07:09,794
C'est la vie.

104
00:07:09,829 --> 00:07:11,896
Why don't you let
me take his place?

105
00:07:11,932 --> 00:07:14,966
I-I know bookkeeping, I know
sales, I know management,

106
00:07:15,002 --> 00:07:16,601
and I know that things

107
00:07:16,636 --> 00:07:19,170
at the pharmaceutical
company weren't exactly kosher,

108
00:07:19,206 --> 00:07:21,473
if you know what I mean.

109
00:07:30,751 --> 00:07:35,286
Yeah, I know what
you're saying, Mr... Long.

110
00:07:35,322 --> 00:07:37,922
In fact, we have been
looking for a replacement

111
00:07:37,957 --> 00:07:40,002
for Michael, and in
view of our situation,

112
00:07:40,026 --> 00:07:42,093
I think you'll do just fine.

113
00:07:42,129 --> 00:07:44,061
Good.

114
00:07:44,097 --> 00:07:46,030
Good. I mean, great.

115
00:07:46,065 --> 00:07:47,477
That's great. That's
just... That's wonderful.

116
00:07:47,501 --> 00:07:48,833
Glad you approve. Yeah.

117
00:07:48,868 --> 00:07:51,413
Well, I think the first thing,
uh, you'll need to do is

118
00:07:51,437 --> 00:07:53,282
take a first-hand
look at our operation,

119
00:07:53,306 --> 00:07:54,672
so, shall we?

120
00:07:54,708 --> 00:07:56,686
Well, you know, I have
been having a hankering

121
00:07:56,710 --> 00:07:58,076
for manicotti lately.

122
00:07:58,111 --> 00:08:00,122
Why don't I...? We'll
keep it warm for you,

123
00:08:00,146 --> 00:08:02,080
Mr. Long.

124
00:08:44,625 --> 00:08:45,991
Who is it?

125
00:08:46,026 --> 00:08:47,492
Mrs. Farrell?

126
00:08:47,527 --> 00:08:50,628
Uh, my name is Ben Matlock.

127
00:08:50,663 --> 00:08:52,464
I'm an attorney.

128
00:08:52,499 --> 00:08:55,600
Well, for crying out
loud, it's about time.

129
00:08:55,635 --> 00:08:57,947
I called you four weeks ago.

130
00:08:57,971 --> 00:08:59,470
You did?

131
00:08:59,506 --> 00:09:01,940
Look, are you gonna help me
sue the phone company or not?

132
00:09:01,975 --> 00:09:04,875
Uh, you want to sue
the phone company?

133
00:09:04,911 --> 00:09:07,211
They keep connecting
me with wrong numbers.

134
00:09:07,247 --> 00:09:09,713
I mean, they-they send me
bills that I can't understand.

135
00:09:09,749 --> 00:09:11,782
Oh, well,

136
00:09:11,818 --> 00:09:14,652
modern life can be
frustrating, I know,

137
00:09:14,687 --> 00:09:18,756
but we can't sue for
everyday aggravations.

138
00:09:21,995 --> 00:09:24,228
What about what
they're doing to my brain?

139
00:09:24,264 --> 00:09:29,100
Uh... uh... What would, uh...

140
00:09:29,135 --> 00:09:30,468
What would that be?

141
00:09:30,504 --> 00:09:32,737
They're tapping in.

142
00:09:32,773 --> 00:09:34,506
They keep...

143
00:09:34,541 --> 00:09:36,408
They keep stealing my thoughts.

144
00:09:38,145 --> 00:09:40,011
Oh.

145
00:09:40,046 --> 00:09:41,947
Wouldn't you want to sue?

146
00:09:41,982 --> 00:09:43,948
Oh, yeah... Oh, yes, I think...

147
00:09:43,984 --> 00:09:46,584
I think I probably...
probably would.

148
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:49,253
Miss-Miss Farrell, in

149
00:09:49,289 --> 00:09:52,557
cases like this,
it's often beneficial

150
00:09:52,592 --> 00:09:55,393
if the attorney
can get statements

151
00:09:55,429 --> 00:09:58,330
from, uh, the next of kin.

152
00:09:58,365 --> 00:10:00,665
I believe you have a daughter.

153
00:10:00,701 --> 00:10:04,101
Maybe I do, and maybe I don't.

154
00:10:04,137 --> 00:10:05,937
What you get me if I do?

155
00:10:05,972 --> 00:10:08,540
Oh, uh...

156
00:10:08,575 --> 00:10:10,820
Uh, help in your
suit against, uh,

157
00:10:10,844 --> 00:10:13,155
the phone company, Mrs. Farrell.

158
00:10:13,179 --> 00:10:16,881
After all, your daughter
would know better

159
00:10:16,916 --> 00:10:21,285
than anyone else how
valuable your thoughts are.

160
00:10:21,321 --> 00:10:23,054
Valuable?

161
00:10:23,089 --> 00:10:25,056
Oh, yes.

162
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:27,069
Somebody tried to
steal your thoughts...

163
00:10:27,093 --> 00:10:28,926
They could owe you a fortune.

164
00:10:31,665 --> 00:10:33,999
Her name is Shelby Russell.

165
00:10:34,034 --> 00:10:35,900
She's a wonderful
girl. She-She calls me

166
00:10:35,936 --> 00:10:37,802
practically every day.

167
00:10:37,838 --> 00:10:39,370
Oh!

168
00:10:44,176 --> 00:10:46,811
Oh, thank you. Thank you.

169
00:10:46,846 --> 00:10:48,780
Thank you.

170
00:10:54,687 --> 00:10:56,954
Hmm.

171
00:12:00,086 --> 00:12:01,119
This is Bruno.

172
00:12:01,154 --> 00:12:02,631
You and he'll have
a nice little chat,

173
00:12:02,655 --> 00:12:03,688
and you'll tell him

174
00:12:03,723 --> 00:12:06,057
exactly what the
hell you're doing here.

175
00:12:06,092 --> 00:12:07,725
Alex.

176
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:10,528
Your driver's license, Alex.

177
00:12:10,563 --> 00:12:13,531
Your credit cards, Alex.

178
00:12:13,566 --> 00:12:18,469
Your American Bar
Association card, Alex.

179
00:12:18,504 --> 00:12:21,472
Your wallet, Alex.

180
00:12:24,677 --> 00:12:28,846
Oh, Bruno, say hello to Alex.

181
00:12:42,462 --> 00:12:44,462
<i>Now, think back.</i>

182
00:12:44,497 --> 00:12:48,466
Didn't anybody ever
call her on the phone,

183
00:12:48,501 --> 00:12:50,969
or leave messages on her door?

184
00:12:51,004 --> 00:12:52,069
Or postcards?

185
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:53,570
Everybody gets postcards.

186
00:12:53,606 --> 00:12:55,572
Uh... How 'bout vacations?

187
00:12:55,608 --> 00:12:56,840
Somewhere she liked to go.

188
00:12:56,876 --> 00:12:58,509
Oh, well, I...

189
00:12:58,544 --> 00:13:01,345
Well, somebody somewhere
has got to know where she is.

190
00:13:04,384 --> 00:13:05,516
Hello?

191
00:13:05,551 --> 00:13:07,196
Stay away from my mother.

192
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:08,352
Shelby?

193
00:13:08,388 --> 00:13:10,366
She has nothing to
do with any of this.

194
00:13:10,390 --> 00:13:12,189
We have to talk.

195
00:13:12,225 --> 00:13:13,690
Forget it.

196
00:13:13,726 --> 00:13:16,027
Shelby, either you talk to me

197
00:13:16,062 --> 00:13:18,029
or your mother talks to me.

198
00:13:18,064 --> 00:13:19,530
Leave her alone, she's ill!

199
00:13:19,565 --> 00:13:21,032
Couldn't you see that?

200
00:13:21,067 --> 00:13:22,866
Clear as day.

201
00:13:22,902 --> 00:13:25,036
Washington Square,
under the arch.

202
00:13:25,071 --> 00:13:27,004
Be there in an hour.

203
00:14:48,154 --> 00:14:50,621
We're running slightly
behind schedule, Mr. Bunabi.

204
00:14:50,656 --> 00:14:52,957
The penicillin is only
now being transferred

205
00:14:52,993 --> 00:14:54,892
to the loading dock. Fine.

206
00:14:54,927 --> 00:14:57,862
It'll give me a chance
to examine your facilities.

207
00:15:08,007 --> 00:15:10,974
This is our quality control.

208
00:15:17,350 --> 00:15:19,495
What's the rate of dilution?

209
00:15:19,519 --> 00:15:22,487
It's 66% saline.

210
00:15:22,522 --> 00:15:25,082
Next shipment, make it 70.

211
00:16:41,734 --> 00:16:43,368
Hello?

212
00:16:43,403 --> 00:16:44,403
You're alone.

213
00:16:44,437 --> 00:16:45,437
That's good.

214
00:16:45,471 --> 00:16:46,637
Where are you?

215
00:16:46,672 --> 00:16:48,650
We'll meet at Bethesda
Fountain in Central Park.

216
00:16:48,674 --> 00:16:51,642
If you're not there in 25
minutes, I won't be either.

217
00:16:56,882 --> 00:16:58,761
Let's go, speed it up!

218
00:16:58,785 --> 00:16:59,795
Do it!

219
00:17:32,752 --> 00:17:35,119
♪ ♪

220
00:17:50,069 --> 00:17:51,268
You all right?

221
00:17:51,304 --> 00:17:52,536
Huh.

222
00:17:52,571 --> 00:17:54,582
I had 'em fooled for
about two minutes.

223
00:17:54,606 --> 00:17:56,584
I still don't know what
they're doing here.

224
00:17:56,608 --> 00:17:58,586
Well, they're diluting
drugs for shipment

225
00:17:58,610 --> 00:17:59,690
to Third World nations.

226
00:18:00,713 --> 00:18:03,480
Tripling their profits.

227
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:07,585
And if we don't get to a
phone in the next ten minutes,

228
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:09,465
all our proof is gonna
be heading for Zaire.

229
00:18:09,489 --> 00:18:11,322
Come on.

230
00:18:22,335 --> 00:18:23,968
A phone.

231
00:18:24,003 --> 00:18:25,970
We need a phone.

232
00:18:26,005 --> 00:18:28,565
Only one I've
seen is over there.

233
00:18:44,823 --> 00:18:47,383
What do you want from me?

234
00:19:02,908 --> 00:19:04,908
Why did you lie to that jury?

235
00:19:04,943 --> 00:19:06,944
Money.

236
00:19:06,979 --> 00:19:09,291
But I don't care
about money now.

237
00:19:09,315 --> 00:19:12,283
My mother's sick and I don't
want anyone bothering her.

238
00:19:12,318 --> 00:19:14,285
Who paid you?

239
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:15,719
I can't tell you.

240
00:19:15,754 --> 00:19:17,688
Edward Kramer?

241
00:19:17,723 --> 00:19:19,723
Thomas Baldwin?

242
00:19:19,758 --> 00:19:22,693
Douglas Clark?

243
00:19:23,762 --> 00:19:26,763
I know lots of
things, Mrs. Russell.

244
00:19:26,799 --> 00:19:30,267
I know you're not Mrs. Russell.

245
00:19:30,302 --> 00:19:34,205
There's no record of
your marriage anywhere.

246
00:19:38,711 --> 00:19:41,271
Michael and I live together.

247
00:19:43,249 --> 00:19:46,217
Our affair started
out as a convenience.

248
00:19:46,252 --> 00:19:49,053
Wealthy men often
like the companionship

249
00:19:49,088 --> 00:19:52,056
of a woman they
like to feel they own.

250
00:19:52,091 --> 00:19:55,059
And you were
rewarded by being...

251
00:19:55,094 --> 00:19:58,062
"Kept" is the word you're
looking for, Mr. Matlock.

252
00:19:58,097 --> 00:20:00,031
Well kept.

253
00:20:02,101 --> 00:20:05,069
Why pretend marriage?

254
00:20:05,104 --> 00:20:08,072
Because Michael wanted to
move in international circles

255
00:20:08,107 --> 00:20:11,075
where casual liaisons
are understood,

256
00:20:11,110 --> 00:20:14,078
but carefully hidden.

257
00:20:14,113 --> 00:20:17,581
As Anthony carefully hid ours.

258
00:20:17,617 --> 00:20:19,583
Michael was possessive

259
00:20:19,618 --> 00:20:22,552
and we felt it was
best that he didn't know.

260
00:20:26,625 --> 00:20:29,459
Shelby...

261
00:20:30,763 --> 00:20:34,431
who paid you to lie
and then disappear?

262
00:20:34,467 --> 00:20:36,033
Who are you protecting?

263
00:20:36,068 --> 00:20:39,036
The meeting's over, Mr. Matlock.

264
00:20:39,071 --> 00:20:41,049
Unless you tell the truth,

265
00:20:41,073 --> 00:20:44,441
Anthony Stewart
will be destroyed.

266
00:20:45,544 --> 00:20:47,778
But if he invokes immunity...

267
00:20:47,813 --> 00:20:50,481
It'll be the end of his career.

268
00:20:50,516 --> 00:20:52,527
If he goes through the trial,

269
00:20:52,551 --> 00:20:55,353
he might very well
be found guilty.

270
00:20:55,388 --> 00:20:58,822
In either case, it'll be
nobody's fault but yours.

271
00:21:03,862 --> 00:21:06,830
I held up my end of the deal.

272
00:21:06,865 --> 00:21:10,000
Now you stay away
from my mother.

273
00:21:10,035 --> 00:21:13,004
Consider this...

274
00:21:13,039 --> 00:21:15,973
the man who paid
you is a murderer.

275
00:21:17,176 --> 00:21:19,154
He already killed Michael.

276
00:21:19,178 --> 00:21:22,346
He tried to kill me.

277
00:21:22,381 --> 00:21:26,183
He came very
near killing Anthony.

278
00:21:26,218 --> 00:21:29,186
You disappear again,

279
00:21:29,222 --> 00:21:32,990
it might very well be forever.

280
00:21:51,143 --> 00:21:53,143
$1 million.

281
00:21:53,178 --> 00:21:55,912
American, as you requested.

282
00:21:55,948 --> 00:21:57,882
Fine.

283
00:22:01,587 --> 00:22:03,565
The party's breaking up,
we gotta do something.

284
00:22:03,589 --> 00:22:05,555
As soon as they're all
looking the other way,

285
00:22:05,591 --> 00:22:07,524
you head for the phone.

286
00:22:09,662 --> 00:22:11,595
Mr. Bunabi.

287
00:22:12,665 --> 00:22:13,864
Hey!

288
00:22:13,899 --> 00:22:15,199
Get him!

289
00:22:30,583 --> 00:22:32,550
I need the police.

290
00:22:32,585 --> 00:22:34,718
There is a crime in progress

291
00:22:34,754 --> 00:22:37,721
in the warehouse
at the end of pier 12.

292
00:22:37,757 --> 00:22:39,723
Hello?

293
00:22:39,759 --> 00:22:41,692
<i>Hello?</i>

294
00:22:46,098 --> 00:22:48,031
Oh.

295
00:23:05,284 --> 00:23:07,284
Bruno.

296
00:23:07,319 --> 00:23:09,253
Whoa!

297
00:23:38,550 --> 00:23:40,484
Oh!

298
00:24:03,942 --> 00:24:05,675
Move.

299
00:25:06,005 --> 00:25:09,017
<i>In the headlines this morning,
three prominent New Yorkers...</i>

300
00:25:09,041 --> 00:25:12,109
<i>Edward Kramer, Thomas
Baldwin and Dr. Douglas Clark...</i>

301
00:25:12,144 --> 00:25:14,844
<i>Were arrested yesterday on
charges of conspiracy and fraud</i>

302
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:17,748
<i>for selling mislabeled
and diluted antibiotics.</i>

303
00:25:17,783 --> 00:25:19,015
<i>In an interesting sidelight,</i>

304
00:25:19,051 --> 00:25:20,995
<i>all three men were
subpoenaed to appear</i>

305
00:25:21,019 --> 00:25:22,564
<i>in English Ambassador
Anthony Stewart's</i>

306
00:25:22,588 --> 00:25:25,956
<i>sensational murder trial,
now entering its third day.</i>

307
00:25:25,991 --> 00:25:28,102
That's right... I was
working my usual shift:

308
00:25:28,126 --> 00:25:30,327
4:00 P.M. to midnight.

309
00:25:30,362 --> 00:25:32,707
Do you recall admitting anyone

310
00:25:32,731 --> 00:25:35,277
to the building at around
10:30 that evening?

311
00:25:35,301 --> 00:25:37,067
I let Mr. Stewart in.

312
00:25:37,102 --> 00:25:38,914
I remember it very well,

313
00:25:38,938 --> 00:25:41,049
because I assumed
that he was going

314
00:25:41,073 --> 00:25:43,607
to see Mrs. Russell,
which I thought was odd,

315
00:25:43,643 --> 00:25:46,443
because I knew
Mr. Russell was up there, too.

316
00:25:46,478 --> 00:25:48,312
You knew about the affair

317
00:25:48,347 --> 00:25:50,525
Mr. Stewart was having
with the decedent's wife?

318
00:25:50,549 --> 00:25:54,017
Being a doorman, seeing
them coming and going,

319
00:25:54,052 --> 00:25:56,220
I had a good idea
something was going on.

320
00:25:56,255 --> 00:25:57,331
Yes.

321
00:25:57,355 --> 00:25:58,567
Did anyone else

322
00:25:58,591 --> 00:26:00,791
visit the Russells'
penthouse that night?

323
00:26:00,826 --> 00:26:02,492
Not that I saw.

324
00:26:02,528 --> 00:26:03,960
You're positive?

325
00:26:03,995 --> 00:26:06,363
I lock the front
door of that building

326
00:26:06,398 --> 00:26:07,831
every night at 8:00.

327
00:26:07,866 --> 00:26:10,178
After that, nobody
can get into the building

328
00:26:10,202 --> 00:26:13,070
unless they have a key
or unless I let them in.

329
00:26:13,105 --> 00:26:15,673
You can get out by going
down the back stairs,

330
00:26:15,708 --> 00:26:17,107
but you can't get in.

331
00:26:17,142 --> 00:26:19,944
But what if they had a key?

332
00:26:19,979 --> 00:26:22,512
What if someone found one or...

333
00:26:22,548 --> 00:26:26,050
borrowed one from a
tenant or had a copy made?

334
00:26:26,085 --> 00:26:29,486
Isn't it possible that someone
other than Mr. Stewart

335
00:26:29,521 --> 00:26:31,055
came into the building,

336
00:26:31,090 --> 00:26:32,734
went up to the
Russells' penthouse

337
00:26:32,758 --> 00:26:34,257
and murdered Michael Russell?

338
00:26:34,293 --> 00:26:35,926
Hey, anything's possible,

339
00:26:35,962 --> 00:26:38,528
but it's my job to
stand at that door

340
00:26:38,564 --> 00:26:40,798
from 4:00 in the
afternoon until midnight.

341
00:26:40,833 --> 00:26:43,000
And if anybody else come in,

342
00:26:43,035 --> 00:26:45,703
especially someone with
a key who wasn't a tenant,

343
00:26:45,738 --> 00:26:47,337
I would have noticed it.

344
00:26:48,407 --> 00:26:50,040
That's all, Mr. Gillespie.

345
00:26:50,075 --> 00:26:51,953
Wish to cross, Mr. Matlock?

346
00:26:51,977 --> 00:26:54,110
Uh... yes.

347
00:26:56,382 --> 00:27:01,952
Uh, you know...
being a... a visitor here,

348
00:27:01,987 --> 00:27:05,522
I can't get over how
much things cost.

349
00:27:05,557 --> 00:27:09,125
I mean, $1.75 for a hot dog,

350
00:27:09,161 --> 00:27:11,194
$12 for a taxi cab ride,

351
00:27:11,229 --> 00:27:14,465
$1,500 for a little
tiny apartment.

352
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:15,932
You live in Manhattan?

353
00:27:15,967 --> 00:27:17,000
Yes, sir.

354
00:27:17,036 --> 00:27:18,869
Well, it must
be pretty difficult

355
00:27:18,904 --> 00:27:21,271
to make ends meet
on a doorman's salary.

356
00:27:21,307 --> 00:27:23,907
Oh, I manage to get
extra work here and there.

357
00:27:23,943 --> 00:27:25,420
Little construction,
some plumbing.

358
00:27:25,444 --> 00:27:28,345
Odd jobs, you
know. Oh, oh, oh, oh.

359
00:27:28,380 --> 00:27:30,091
Oh, do... do you
do any work for...

360
00:27:30,115 --> 00:27:32,115
for anyone in the
apartment building?

361
00:27:32,417 --> 00:27:34,051
Oh, yeah, now and then.

362
00:27:34,086 --> 00:27:35,118
Mm-hmm.

363
00:27:35,153 --> 00:27:37,253
How about the Rothbergs in 12D?

364
00:27:37,289 --> 00:27:38,655
You ever do anything for them?

365
00:27:38,691 --> 00:27:39,890
What was that, sir?

366
00:27:39,925 --> 00:27:42,759
I say the Rothbergs in 12D.

367
00:27:42,795 --> 00:27:44,695
I've probably done something

368
00:27:44,730 --> 00:27:47,197
for everybody in the building
at one time or another.

369
00:27:48,267 --> 00:27:50,767
Uh, uh, Mr. Gillespie,

370
00:27:50,802 --> 00:27:54,104
uh, I... I think, in
all the excitement,

371
00:27:54,139 --> 00:27:57,574
uh, you're overlooking
something unusual

372
00:27:57,609 --> 00:27:58,942
that happened the night

373
00:27:58,977 --> 00:28:01,345
Michael Russell was murdered.

374
00:28:01,380 --> 00:28:03,614
Did, uh,

375
00:28:03,649 --> 00:28:07,484
Mrs. Rothberg ask you
to take her dog for a walk?

376
00:28:09,188 --> 00:28:11,288
Yes, yes, she did, yes. Uh-huh.

377
00:28:11,323 --> 00:28:13,223
And did you take
her dog for a walk?

378
00:28:13,258 --> 00:28:14,691
That's right.

379
00:28:14,726 --> 00:28:16,137
So many other things
happened that night,

380
00:28:16,161 --> 00:28:17,605
I forgot about it.
Oh, sure, sure.

381
00:28:17,629 --> 00:28:20,530
It's not as if you
did it every night.

382
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:24,234
At what time would
that have been?

383
00:28:24,269 --> 00:28:25,936
About 10:15.

384
00:28:25,971 --> 00:28:26,936
Mm-hmm.

385
00:28:26,972 --> 00:28:28,132
And how long were you gone?

386
00:28:29,208 --> 00:28:31,341
Ten, 15 minutes.

387
00:28:31,376 --> 00:28:32,442
Mm-hmm.

388
00:28:32,477 --> 00:28:34,444
So, it is possible someone

389
00:28:34,479 --> 00:28:37,681
could have entered the
building without your seeing them.

390
00:28:38,917 --> 00:28:40,550
I'm afraid so.

391
00:28:41,854 --> 00:28:42,953
Thank you.

392
00:28:42,988 --> 00:28:44,587
No further questions.

393
00:28:44,623 --> 00:28:45,655
Redirect?

394
00:28:45,690 --> 00:28:47,724
No, Your Honor.

395
00:28:48,794 --> 00:28:51,395
Mr. Gillespie, you're excused.

396
00:28:53,465 --> 00:28:56,111
Due to the lateness of the hour,

397
00:28:56,135 --> 00:28:57,434
court is adjourned

398
00:28:57,469 --> 00:29:00,069
until 10:00 A.M.
tomorrow morning.

399
00:29:09,949 --> 00:29:12,716
What was all that about?

400
00:29:12,751 --> 00:29:15,385
He walks that dog
every night at 9:30.

401
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:17,320
He was lying to save his job.

402
00:29:17,355 --> 00:29:19,056
You protected him.

403
00:29:19,091 --> 00:29:22,158
I just made it easier
for him to tell the truth.

404
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:49,864
Thanks for the note.

405
00:29:49,888 --> 00:29:52,033
Does this mean you'll testify?

406
00:29:52,057 --> 00:29:53,957
I can't.

407
00:29:53,992 --> 00:29:55,959
I'm scared.

408
00:29:55,994 --> 00:29:58,394
Everything you said
is true, Mr. Matlock.

409
00:29:58,430 --> 00:29:59,974
He'll eventually
get rid of me, too.

410
00:29:59,998 --> 00:30:01,198
It's just a matter of time.

411
00:30:01,233 --> 00:30:02,733
Who?

412
00:30:02,768 --> 00:30:04,134
A deal first.

413
00:30:04,169 --> 00:30:06,370
You give me immunity;
I give you the killer.

414
00:30:06,405 --> 00:30:08,471
I can't give immunity.

415
00:30:08,507 --> 00:30:10,451
That's up to the
district attorney.

416
00:30:10,475 --> 00:30:12,175
But I promise I'll talk to him,

417
00:30:12,210 --> 00:30:13,955
get him to go as
easy as possible.

418
00:30:13,979 --> 00:30:16,680
Just tell me who the killer is.

419
00:30:16,715 --> 00:30:18,415
I shouldn't have come.

420
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:26,055
Edward Kramer.

421
00:30:26,091 --> 00:30:28,425
There's a ditch behind
his home in the country.

422
00:30:28,460 --> 00:30:30,538
That's where you'll
find the murder weapon.

423
00:30:30,562 --> 00:30:32,529
How do you know?

424
00:30:32,565 --> 00:30:35,098
I heard him say that's
where he dumped it.

425
00:30:52,484 --> 00:30:55,164
Hey, hey, what
are you doing here?

426
00:30:55,887 --> 00:30:57,865
I have a search warrant
for you, Mr. Kramer.

427
00:30:57,889 --> 00:30:59,134
For what?

428
00:30:59,158 --> 00:31:01,369
Hey, Lieutenant?

429
00:31:01,393 --> 00:31:02,393
Lieutenant!

430
00:31:20,912 --> 00:31:23,213
Another week or two, this
area would be flooded out

431
00:31:23,248 --> 00:31:24,681
and we never
would have found it.

432
00:31:24,716 --> 00:31:27,550
How stupid do you think I am?

433
00:31:48,806 --> 00:31:51,174
Well, what happened?

434
00:31:51,209 --> 00:31:53,176
Everything matches.

435
00:31:53,211 --> 00:31:55,145
The blood on the
candlestick holder

436
00:31:55,180 --> 00:31:57,280
matches Michael Russell's blood.

437
00:31:57,315 --> 00:31:58,849
The size and shape

438
00:31:58,884 --> 00:32:01,051
of his wound matched
the size and shape

439
00:32:01,086 --> 00:32:02,452
of the candlestick.

440
00:32:02,487 --> 00:32:04,354
The candlestick matches the one

441
00:32:04,389 --> 00:32:06,890
that's still in the penthouse.

442
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:08,591
Then, it's over.

443
00:32:08,626 --> 00:32:10,293
It appears to be.

444
00:32:10,329 --> 00:32:12,428
It<i> appears</i> to be?

445
00:32:12,464 --> 00:32:14,764
I'm going to call the embassy.

446
00:32:21,472 --> 00:32:23,372
What's the matter?

447
00:32:26,277 --> 00:32:28,411
That fellow, Kramer,

448
00:32:28,446 --> 00:32:32,548
seem to be particularly
stupid to you?

449
00:32:32,584 --> 00:32:34,350
No.

450
00:32:44,129 --> 00:32:46,330
♪ ♪

451
00:33:04,282 --> 00:33:06,049
We might as well
have a little fun.

452
00:33:06,084 --> 00:33:08,652
Yeah.

453
00:33:08,687 --> 00:33:10,164
Ladies and gentlemen,

454
00:33:10,188 --> 00:33:12,255
it means a great deal to me

455
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:14,657
not only to celebrate
this moment,

456
00:33:14,692 --> 00:33:16,926
but to do so with friends.

457
00:33:16,961 --> 00:33:18,894
And one of my best friends

458
00:33:18,930 --> 00:33:21,197
is my attorney, Mr. Ben Matlock.

459
00:33:23,469 --> 00:33:25,835
Where are you, Ben?

460
00:33:25,870 --> 00:33:27,837
Come up here and
say a few words.

461
00:33:27,872 --> 00:33:29,150
Don't be shy.

462
00:33:30,909 --> 00:33:33,469
Hear! Hear!

463
00:33:40,919 --> 00:33:42,919
Well, I, uh,

464
00:33:42,954 --> 00:33:47,023
um, I really don't
have a lot to say.

465
00:33:47,058 --> 00:33:50,260
I, I haven't done very much.

466
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:54,831
It's, it's almost as
if everything was

467
00:33:54,867 --> 00:33:57,267
handed to me, uh,

468
00:33:57,302 --> 00:33:59,936
on a silver platter as they say.

469
00:34:04,510 --> 00:34:09,012
Um... well, we still
have court tomorrow.

470
00:34:10,182 --> 00:34:11,348
No time for speeches.

471
00:34:11,383 --> 00:34:12,516
Have fun. Have fun.

472
00:34:12,551 --> 00:34:14,518
Thank you.

473
00:34:37,876 --> 00:34:39,116
Here, is this what you wanted?

474
00:34:39,144 --> 00:34:41,355
Yeah. Yeah, yeah, thanks.

475
00:34:41,379 --> 00:34:43,313
Thanks.

476
00:34:50,889 --> 00:34:52,255
Hey, Mac, what are you doing?

477
00:34:52,290 --> 00:34:54,024
That's evidence
you're defacing there.

478
00:34:54,059 --> 00:34:55,492
What's the matter with you?

479
00:34:56,895 --> 00:34:58,828
Thanks.

480
00:35:27,025 --> 00:35:30,026
He's going to make
the motion to dismiss.

481
00:35:30,062 --> 00:35:34,664
But... I don't want
him to dismiss.

482
00:35:34,699 --> 00:35:36,333
What?

483
00:35:36,368 --> 00:35:38,935
If he makes the motion
and he dismisses,

484
00:35:38,971 --> 00:35:40,370
you'll have a cloud

485
00:35:40,405 --> 00:35:41,938
over your head
the rest of your life.

486
00:35:41,974 --> 00:35:44,074
You don't want that, do you?

487
00:35:47,045 --> 00:35:50,780
All rise! The Court of
the state of New York

488
00:35:50,815 --> 00:35:53,450
for the county of New
York is now in session.

489
00:35:53,485 --> 00:35:56,186
The Honorable
Raymond Kawai presiding.

490
00:35:57,990 --> 00:35:59,956
Be seated.

491
00:35:59,992 --> 00:36:03,126
I understand the prosecution
would like to make a motion.

492
00:36:03,161 --> 00:36:05,328
Your Honor?

493
00:36:05,363 --> 00:36:08,164
Before any motions are made,

494
00:36:08,200 --> 00:36:11,734
our commitment to justice
compels me to ask the court

495
00:36:11,769 --> 00:36:15,639
to permit the defense to
call one additional witness.

496
00:36:15,674 --> 00:36:17,573
Counsel?

497
00:36:19,010 --> 00:36:21,722
No objection. Call your
witness, Mr. Matlock.

498
00:36:21,746 --> 00:36:24,581
Defense calls Dr. Douglas Clark.

499
00:36:24,616 --> 00:36:27,484
Dr. Clark, you were a partner

500
00:36:27,519 --> 00:36:29,730
in the Russell
Pharmaceutical Company,

501
00:36:29,754 --> 00:36:31,665
is that right? Yes, I was.

502
00:36:31,689 --> 00:36:33,301
And who were the other partners?

503
00:36:33,325 --> 00:36:35,725
Thomas Baldwin
and Edward Kramer.

504
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:37,672
And of course, the
late Michael Russell.

505
00:36:37,696 --> 00:36:39,395
He ran the whole thing.

506
00:36:39,430 --> 00:36:42,298
Uh, is there any truth

507
00:36:42,333 --> 00:36:45,501
to the allegation
that your company

508
00:36:45,536 --> 00:36:48,838
was peddling
watered-down antibiotics

509
00:36:48,873 --> 00:36:51,341
to unsuspecting
Third World nations?

510
00:36:52,944 --> 00:36:54,277
On the advice of counsel,

511
00:36:54,312 --> 00:36:56,346
I invoke my Fifth
Amendment rights

512
00:36:56,381 --> 00:36:58,615
and, uh, refuse to
answer that question.

513
00:36:58,650 --> 00:37:01,317
You know, the fact
that you won't say

514
00:37:01,353 --> 00:37:02,885
whether you were selling

515
00:37:02,920 --> 00:37:05,488
watered-down antibiotics
suggests that you were.

516
00:37:05,523 --> 00:37:08,525
Mr. Matlock, your question

517
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:10,693
is irrelevant, argumentative,

518
00:37:10,728 --> 00:37:14,431
improper and unconstitutional.

519
00:37:14,466 --> 00:37:16,778
In fact, it's not
even a question.

520
00:37:16,802 --> 00:37:18,902
The court is sustaining

521
00:37:18,937 --> 00:37:21,437
it's own objections,
and admonishing you

522
00:37:21,473 --> 00:37:23,539
to limit this examination

523
00:37:23,574 --> 00:37:25,286
to the issues in this trial.

524
00:37:25,310 --> 00:37:27,410
Yes, sir, Your
Honor, I apologize.

525
00:37:27,445 --> 00:37:30,746
I was just trying to get
the truth any way I can.

526
00:37:30,781 --> 00:37:32,448
Uh, withdrawn.

527
00:37:32,484 --> 00:37:36,785
Uh, was, uh, the Russell
Pharmaceutical Company, uh...

528
00:37:36,821 --> 00:37:38,788
uh, profitable?

529
00:37:38,823 --> 00:37:40,823
Yes, it was.

530
00:37:40,859 --> 00:37:42,158
That's why I invested in it.

531
00:37:42,193 --> 00:37:45,128
But you have a lot of
money problems, don't you?

532
00:37:45,163 --> 00:37:46,963
No, I don't.

533
00:37:46,998 --> 00:37:48,876
Are you kidding? You're up

534
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:51,167
to your ears in debt.

535
00:37:51,203 --> 00:37:54,503
You owe $2 million

536
00:37:54,539 --> 00:37:56,606
on your Park Avenue co-op,

537
00:37:56,641 --> 00:37:59,742
$800,000 on your
Fifth Avenue offices,

538
00:37:59,777 --> 00:38:02,256
and about that same amount

539
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:04,214
on the East Hampton
summer house.

540
00:38:04,249 --> 00:38:06,460
Those figures sound about right?

541
00:38:06,484 --> 00:38:08,362
I don't know. I would have
to check with my accountant.

542
00:38:08,386 --> 00:38:10,286
I don't know what you call

543
00:38:10,322 --> 00:38:13,423
being $3 1/2 million in debt,

544
00:38:13,458 --> 00:38:15,325
but myself and anybody I know

545
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:17,104
would call "that
turning the page

546
00:38:17,128 --> 00:38:19,274
on Chapter 11,"
wouldn't you, Carl?

547
00:38:19,298 --> 00:38:21,298
Excuse me, is this
entirely necessary?

548
00:38:21,333 --> 00:38:25,301
Uh, you, uh, you're from
New York, aren't you?

549
00:38:26,405 --> 00:38:27,804
Yes.

550
00:38:27,839 --> 00:38:30,640
Any, uh, other relatives?

551
00:38:30,676 --> 00:38:36,312
My mother. And
she resides at the...

552
00:38:36,348 --> 00:38:39,849
Miles Carson Convalescent Home,

553
00:38:39,884 --> 00:38:43,986
because of some mental problems.

554
00:38:44,021 --> 00:38:46,155
And her name is Elizabeth...?

555
00:38:46,190 --> 00:38:48,024
Farrell. "Farrell."

556
00:38:48,059 --> 00:38:50,360
She changed her name from
Clark when she remarried.

557
00:38:50,395 --> 00:38:52,094
Yeah, yeah.

558
00:38:52,130 --> 00:38:54,997
You know, I went to see
your mother last night.

559
00:38:55,033 --> 00:38:59,168
And it just... It took forever

560
00:38:59,204 --> 00:39:03,006
for her to admit
that you're her son.

561
00:39:03,041 --> 00:39:06,609
She has a paranoia where she,

562
00:39:06,644 --> 00:39:10,179
uh, she believes the
telephone company

563
00:39:10,215 --> 00:39:14,450
plugged in to her mind and
they can read her thoughts.

564
00:39:14,486 --> 00:39:16,952
And you told her

565
00:39:16,988 --> 00:39:19,788
that if she told anybody
you were her son,

566
00:39:19,824 --> 00:39:21,824
the phone company'd
find out about it.

567
00:39:23,161 --> 00:39:25,106
Why didn't you want
anybody to know?

568
00:39:25,130 --> 00:39:28,131
Well, it's, uh, what you said.

569
00:39:28,166 --> 00:39:31,567
She has these mental problems,
and I'm trying to protect her.

570
00:39:31,602 --> 00:39:33,269
I don't think so.

571
00:39:33,304 --> 00:39:39,976
I don't think the connection
between you and your mother

572
00:39:40,011 --> 00:39:42,511
is what you don't want
people to find out about.

573
00:39:42,547 --> 00:39:46,149
I think it's the
connection between you

574
00:39:46,184 --> 00:39:47,550
and your sister.

575
00:39:50,087 --> 00:39:52,856
Your sister is Shelby
Russell, isn't she?

576
00:39:52,891 --> 00:39:54,857
Yes.

577
00:39:54,892 --> 00:39:56,492
Now!

578
00:39:56,528 --> 00:39:58,795
Now, we're coming
to the good part.

579
00:40:00,198 --> 00:40:03,366
This whole crooked
pharmaceutical scheme

580
00:40:03,401 --> 00:40:04,612
was your idea, wasn't it?

581
00:40:04,636 --> 00:40:06,336
I object. Allegedly crooked.

582
00:40:06,371 --> 00:40:08,471
Allegedly crooked. Huh?

583
00:40:09,941 --> 00:40:12,242
You needed money.

584
00:40:12,277 --> 00:40:16,779
You have connections
with legitimate

585
00:40:16,814 --> 00:40:18,526
drug manufacturers;
all you needed

586
00:40:18,550 --> 00:40:20,994
was your scheme and a front man.

587
00:40:21,018 --> 00:40:22,963
Someone to run the business

588
00:40:22,987 --> 00:40:26,522
and to keep your
involvement quiet.

589
00:40:26,558 --> 00:40:27,957
Michael Russell.

590
00:40:27,992 --> 00:40:30,026
The business was Michael's idea.

591
00:40:30,061 --> 00:40:31,427
He approached us with it.

592
00:40:31,462 --> 00:40:35,431
I'll bet if we could
speak to Michael

593
00:40:35,466 --> 00:40:36,865
in the Great Unknown,

594
00:40:36,901 --> 00:40:39,235
he would tell us
it was your idea!

595
00:40:39,270 --> 00:40:42,137
I object. I object myself.
That was very corny.

596
00:40:42,173 --> 00:40:44,540
Let's, let's change the subject.

597
00:40:44,576 --> 00:40:47,277
You didn't really get along
with Michael, did you?

598
00:40:47,312 --> 00:40:50,012
You didn't trust him.

599
00:40:50,047 --> 00:40:53,583
To the point that
your sister, Shelby,

600
00:40:53,618 --> 00:40:56,452
moved in with him

601
00:40:56,488 --> 00:40:58,888
and actually
pretended to be his wife.

602
00:40:58,923 --> 00:41:01,291
All to look after your
interests, isn't that right?

603
00:41:01,326 --> 00:41:02,759
That's absolutely absurd.

604
00:41:02,794 --> 00:41:06,154
She lived with him because
she loved him. "Loved him"?

605
00:41:06,430 --> 00:41:08,697
"Loved him"?

606
00:41:09,667 --> 00:41:12,201
Well, if, if she loved him,

607
00:41:12,237 --> 00:41:16,772
why did she have the hot
affair with Anthony Stewart?

608
00:41:16,807 --> 00:41:18,952
If Mr. Matlock is
making a point,

609
00:41:18,976 --> 00:41:20,776
I'd wish he get to it.

610
00:41:20,812 --> 00:41:22,523
I wouldn't mind that myself.

611
00:41:22,547 --> 00:41:24,358
Oh, I'm getting
to it, Your Honor.

612
00:41:24,382 --> 00:41:26,649
I'm getting to it.

613
00:41:26,685 --> 00:41:28,918
Something went wrong

614
00:41:28,953 --> 00:41:31,854
between you and Michael.

615
00:41:31,889 --> 00:41:33,189
You got too greedy.

616
00:41:33,224 --> 00:41:34,857
He got too greedy...

617
00:41:34,893 --> 00:41:37,427
Anyway, you went
up there that night.

618
00:41:37,462 --> 00:41:40,663
Mr. Gillespie was walking
Mrs. Rothberg's dog.

619
00:41:40,698 --> 00:41:41,942
He didn't see you
enter the building.

620
00:41:41,966 --> 00:41:43,232
You had a key.

621
00:41:43,268 --> 00:41:44,967
Shelby's.

622
00:41:45,003 --> 00:41:47,870
You let yourself in,
went up to the penthouse.

623
00:41:47,906 --> 00:41:49,038
Confronted Michael.

624
00:41:49,073 --> 00:41:50,173
Argued with him.

625
00:41:50,208 --> 00:41:51,740
Fought with him.

626
00:41:51,776 --> 00:41:52,886
Took the candlestick

627
00:41:52,910 --> 00:41:54,355
from the mantelpiece
and killed him.

628
00:41:54,379 --> 00:41:55,344
How am I doing?

629
00:41:55,379 --> 00:41:56,546
You're dead wrong!

630
00:41:56,581 --> 00:42:00,116
Now, comes your
most imaginative idea.

631
00:42:00,151 --> 00:42:02,085
The most imaginative.

632
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:03,486
Michael's dead.

633
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:05,955
Now, what do you do?

634
00:42:07,192 --> 00:42:09,825
You and your
sister frame the man

635
00:42:09,861 --> 00:42:12,161
she's been having
the affair with,

636
00:42:12,197 --> 00:42:13,729
the ambassador.

637
00:42:13,764 --> 00:42:16,277
He'll claim diplomatic immunity

638
00:42:16,301 --> 00:42:17,511
and go to England.

639
00:42:17,535 --> 00:42:18,868
It's all over.

640
00:42:18,903 --> 00:42:20,903
The only problem:

641
00:42:20,938 --> 00:42:23,739
Anthony is an honorable man.

642
00:42:23,774 --> 00:42:26,075
He wants to go to trial.

643
00:42:26,111 --> 00:42:28,344
He wants to be found innocent.

644
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:30,513
Now, what do you do?

645
00:42:30,548 --> 00:42:33,316
Well... well, hell!

646
00:42:35,053 --> 00:42:38,321
You just find
somebody else to frame.

647
00:42:38,356 --> 00:42:40,423
The original murder
weapon is gone,

648
00:42:40,458 --> 00:42:43,192
probably somewhere
in the East River.

649
00:42:43,228 --> 00:42:45,028
You find a duplicate.

650
00:42:45,063 --> 00:42:47,909
Smear it as it were
with Michael's blood.

651
00:42:47,933 --> 00:42:49,977
Plant it on
Mr. Kramer's property.

652
00:42:50,001 --> 00:42:52,446
Have your sister notify
the proper person,

653
00:42:52,470 --> 00:42:53,569
me.

654
00:42:53,605 --> 00:42:55,483
Your Honor, this
is all conjecture.

655
00:42:55,507 --> 00:42:57,818
Your Honor, a
moment more, please.

656
00:42:57,842 --> 00:42:59,820
I can prove. I have proof.

657
00:42:59,844 --> 00:43:01,377
Very well, but you'd better.

658
00:43:03,681 --> 00:43:11,120
Now... according to
the coroner's report,

659
00:43:11,155 --> 00:43:16,359
Michael Russell was a diabetic.

660
00:43:16,394 --> 00:43:19,429
What does that mean
to you as a doctor?

661
00:43:19,464 --> 00:43:22,164
Well, it can mean
many things. What?

662
00:43:22,200 --> 00:43:23,944
Well, he'd have to
have insulin, for one.

663
00:43:23,968 --> 00:43:25,846
Every day? Yes.

664
00:43:25,870 --> 00:43:29,171
Would that mean
he'd need syringes?

665
00:43:29,207 --> 00:43:31,274
Bottles of insulin

666
00:43:31,309 --> 00:43:33,353
for his daily injections?

667
00:43:33,377 --> 00:43:35,845
Pharmaceutical bottles

668
00:43:35,880 --> 00:43:42,385
with labels with his name
and the doctor's name?

669
00:43:44,488 --> 00:43:46,823
You were the
doctor, weren't you?

670
00:43:46,858 --> 00:43:48,691
No.

671
00:43:48,726 --> 00:43:50,793
Mr. Matlock, is this
going any further?

672
00:43:50,828 --> 00:43:52,395
Is there proof?

673
00:43:52,430 --> 00:43:53,863
Yes, sir, Your Honor.

674
00:43:55,233 --> 00:43:58,701
I'm showing the witness
People's Exhibit 15.

675
00:43:58,737 --> 00:44:01,404
The alleged murder weapon.

676
00:44:01,439 --> 00:44:03,973
Ask you to look on the
bottom there. What do you see?

677
00:44:04,008 --> 00:44:05,340
Looks like a scratch.

678
00:44:05,376 --> 00:44:06,742
It is a scratch.

679
00:44:06,777 --> 00:44:08,978
I made it myself last night.

680
00:44:09,013 --> 00:44:10,824
Scratched it right
down to the bare metal

681
00:44:10,848 --> 00:44:12,948
what this is made of: brass.

682
00:44:14,852 --> 00:44:17,319
This isn't silver.

683
00:44:17,354 --> 00:44:18,821
This is silver-plate.

684
00:44:18,856 --> 00:44:21,423
The one up in
Michael's apartment

685
00:44:21,458 --> 00:44:22,669
is solid silver.

686
00:44:22,693 --> 00:44:25,128
This is not.

687
00:44:26,197 --> 00:44:28,598
You know what that means?

688
00:44:28,633 --> 00:44:31,000
This is not the murder weapon.

689
00:44:31,035 --> 00:44:33,269
The police found
that candlestick.

690
00:44:33,304 --> 00:44:34,982
And when they did, it
was completely covered

691
00:44:35,006 --> 00:44:36,316
with Michael's
blood. That's right!

692
00:44:36,340 --> 00:44:39,174
And you had removed
all the syringes,

693
00:44:39,210 --> 00:44:42,344
all the insulin from
Michael's apartment,

694
00:44:42,380 --> 00:44:45,982
so that no one would
ever see your name.

695
00:44:47,318 --> 00:44:54,189
But sooner or later, someone
was bound to stumble...

696
00:44:54,225 --> 00:44:55,958
onto this.

697
00:44:57,394 --> 00:45:01,330
Would you tell the
court what this is?

698
00:45:03,835 --> 00:45:05,679
That's a receipt
for a blood bank.

699
00:45:05,703 --> 00:45:06,980
This is where the sample

700
00:45:07,004 --> 00:45:10,573
of Michael Russell's
blood was kept,

701
00:45:10,608 --> 00:45:16,612
20 ccs... of type
O negative blood.

702
00:45:18,950 --> 00:45:22,785
That was removed on
October 19, two days ago.

703
00:45:24,588 --> 00:45:26,667
You can tell it's
signed by a doctor.

704
00:45:26,691 --> 00:45:29,203
You can't read the signature.

705
00:45:29,227 --> 00:45:32,161
Now, we can have
an expert tell us.

706
00:45:32,197 --> 00:45:35,164
But since you're
sitting right here,

707
00:45:35,200 --> 00:45:37,099
want to take a
shot at telling us

708
00:45:37,134 --> 00:45:38,967
who's signature that is?

709
00:45:42,874 --> 00:45:44,507
It's mine.

710
00:45:44,542 --> 00:45:46,341
That's right.

711
00:45:46,377 --> 00:45:48,310
It's yours.

712
00:45:52,050 --> 00:45:54,517
Well, that's about it.

713
00:45:54,552 --> 00:45:57,753
Your Honor,

714
00:45:57,789 --> 00:46:00,857
I believe there is
reasonable doubt

715
00:46:00,892 --> 00:46:04,259
that my client is
guilty of any crime.

716
00:46:05,363 --> 00:46:07,696
I believe the court,

717
00:46:07,731 --> 00:46:10,199
the district attorney and I

718
00:46:10,234 --> 00:46:14,470
have fulfilled our
commitment to justice.

719
00:46:14,505 --> 00:46:18,474
And I now ask that
this case be dismissed.

720
00:46:18,509 --> 00:46:20,387
Case dismissed.

721
00:46:33,357 --> 00:46:34,824
Compliments of
the British Embassy.

722
00:46:34,859 --> 00:46:37,927
Nothing like traveling
home in style.

723
00:46:37,962 --> 00:46:40,162
Well, now, if you ever
get back to New York,

724
00:46:40,197 --> 00:46:42,598
you know where I am.
You really like it here,

725
00:46:42,633 --> 00:46:43,944
don't you? Oh, yeah.

726
00:46:43,968 --> 00:46:45,167
It's the Big Apple, Ben.

727
00:46:45,202 --> 00:46:46,847
Anything that happens
in the whole world,

728
00:46:46,871 --> 00:46:49,171
happens here first.

729
00:46:49,206 --> 00:46:50,606
Don't you fell the energy?!

730
00:46:50,641 --> 00:46:52,274
No.

731
00:46:53,544 --> 00:46:55,756
Take care. Well...
listen, if you ever

732
00:46:55,780 --> 00:46:58,548
feel like unplugging
yourself from all this energy,

733
00:46:58,583 --> 00:47:00,427
stop by Atlanta sometime.

734
00:47:00,451 --> 00:47:02,251
Okay, thank you. I will. Okay.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
